const interviewScenarios = <String, String>{
  'Python': r"""
Beginner | Values | What does bool('False') return? | It returns True because a string is truthy whenever it contains at least one character. Parse accepted string values explicitly when they represent flags. | Python checks whether the string is empty. | Validate a configuration setting against {'true': True, 'false': False} after stripping and lowercasing.
Intermediate | Function arguments | Why does a function with a default list accumulate values between calls? | Defaults are evaluated when the function is defined. Calls without an explicit list receive the same object. Use None as the default and allocate a fresh list inside the function. | A mutable default belongs to the function object. | def add(value, items=None): return [value] if items is None else [*items, value]
Advanced | Memory profiling | A batch job's resident memory grows after deleting results. How do you isolate the cause? | Compare Python allocation snapshots with RSS over identical batches. Inspect retained references if Python allocations grow. Investigate extension allocations or allocator retention if RSS alone grows. | Process memory and live Python objects measure different things. | Compare tracemalloc snapshots every 100 batches while tracking process RSS.
Beginner | Numeric types | Why does 0.1 + 0.2 differ from 0.3 in a Python equality test? | Most decimal fractions cannot be represented exactly as binary floating-point numbers. The operands and sum are rounded. Use math.isclose for suitable approximate comparisons or Decimal with string inputs for decimal arithmetic. | Binary floating point approximates these fractions. | math.isclose(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3)
Beginner | Unpacking | What is returned by first, *middle, last = [4, 5, 6, 7]? | first is 4, middle is the list [5, 6], and last is 7. The starred target absorbs all remaining items after the fixed positions are assigned. | Reserve the endpoints and collect everything between them. | Use header, *data, footer = lines when the outer records have different roles.
Intermediate | File processing | How would you process a 10 GB line-delimited JSON export? | Open the source as a text stream, iterate line by line, decode each JSON value and write results incrementally. Record invalid lines and checkpoint only if reruns require it. | Read and process one record at a time. | Iterate for line in source and parse each with json.loads(line).
Intermediate | Test design | How do you test code that depends on the current time without sleeping? | Pass a clock or explicit timestamp into the business logic, then supply known times around relevant boundaries. Test integration with the real clock separately. | Control time as a test input. | Use an injected clock that returns 23:59 UTC and then midnight.
Advanced | Resource cleanup | A cancelled asyncio task leaves an open connection. Where should cleanup live? | Bind connection lifetime to an async context manager or a finally block. Ensure acquisition and release tolerate cancellation and allow cancellation to propagate after cleanup. | Cancellation exits through normal cleanup paths. | async with pool.acquire() as conn: await conn.fetch(query)
Advanced | Imports | A package works as a module but fails when its file is executed directly. How do you diagnose it? | Compare module names, package context and import paths using the supported invocation. Put reusable logic in the package and expose a module entry point or installed command. Avoid dependence on the working directory. | Python resolves relative imports through package context. | Run python -m reports.export rather than python reports/export.py.
""",
  'SQL': r"""
Beginner | Aggregation | What do COUNT(*) and COUNT(amount) return for amounts 5, NULL and 0? | COUNT(*) returns 3 because it counts all input rows. COUNT(amount) returns 2 because it ignores NULL values and includes zero. | One counts rows. The other counts known values. | Compare the two counts to quantify missing amounts in an import.
Intermediate | Window functions | How can you retain exactly the most recent invoice per customer? | Assign ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY issued_at DESC, invoice_id DESC) and filter to row 1 in an outer query. The unique tie-breaker makes equal timestamps deterministic. | Number each customer's invoices from newest to oldest. | Two invoices issued at noon produce one winner based on invoice ID.
Advanced | Query tuning | Why might a previously fast query be slow for a high-volume tenant? | Compare actual and estimated row counts and execution plans. Check tenant skew, plan reuse, relevant indexes and projected rows. Validate changes with representative data and measured reads. | A plan suited to a small tenant can be expensive for a large one. | Compare actual plans for 20 rows and 2 million rows using the same tenant predicate.
Beginner | Sorting | Why does fetching the first five rows without ORDER BY produce different results? | A relation has no inherent presentation order. Changes in plans, physical layout or concurrent writes may alter encounter order. Apply ORDER BY with a unique tie-breaker before limiting rows. | Specify which rows count as first. | ORDER BY created_at DESC, order_id DESC LIMIT 5
Intermediate | Anti joins | Find customers without paid orders even when orders contains NULL customer IDs. | Use a correlated NOT EXISTS subquery matching the customer key and paid status. A NULL key fails the equality condition and does not affect other customer rows. | Keep customers only when no matching paid order exists. | SELECT c.id FROM customers c WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id=c.id AND o.status='paid');
Advanced | Transactions | Two workers schedule overlapping shifts after both see an empty calendar. How do you prevent the race? | Enforce the overlap invariant with an exclusion constraint where supported, or serialize writers on a shared resource row. Serializable transactions with appropriate retries can also protect predicate reads where the engine supports them. | Coordinate the check and insert so they cannot both succeed incorrectly. | In PostgreSQL, exclude overlapping time ranges for each employee with a suitable GiST constraint.
""",
  'Machine Learning': r"""
